add('lesson-builder__cn26_09',
'''Assume senior-secondary pupils know sequence notation. Convergence means that for each positive tolerance, every sufficiently late term lies within that tolerance of one fixed limit. Inspecting a few initial terms is not enough.
The sequence 1/n converges to 0: for a tolerance epsilon, choose N>1/epsilon. The sequence (-1)^n/n also converges to 0 because its absolute distance is 1/n, although it alternates and is not monotone. The bounded sequence (-1)^n does not converge: its even and odd tails stay at different values. Thus boundedness alone is not sufficient for convergence.
Begin with a table and a number-line strip around zero, ask when all remaining terms stay inside, and then translate this into the tail condition. Use the alternating examples to test which visual intuitions are unreliable. As a transfer check, ask whether 2+(-1)^n/n converges and why; its limit is 2 by the same distance argument. If a pupil uses bounded as the entire explanation, ask them to test (-1)^n against a narrow strip around a proposed limit. These are mathematical examples, not a claim about actual class performance.''',
'Thus boundedness alone is not sufficient for convergence.','Thus boundedness alone is sufficient for convergence.', ['C5'],
'''Every bounded sequence converges. Students should memorize this, and they never need to check what happens to sufficiently late terms. Being monotone is also necessary in every convergent sequence.''')

add('adaptive-hint-sequence-designer__cn53_05',
'''I would first acknowledge the pupil's effort: “Cloudiness tells us something about this model; let's investigate one thing rather than rebuild everything.” I have not seen the device, so I would not name a certain cause. This is a classroom demonstration, not a drinking-water treatment system. Even clear water from this filter is not guaranteed safe to drink; no one should taste it.
Ask the pupil to draw the flow path and compare the water before and after filtering against the same light background. If they cannot choose a next step, prompt them to consider one testable possibility such as water bypassing a layer or particles coming from unwashed material. With teacher-approved materials, keep the water sample and volume similar and change only one arrangement or preparation factor. Do not tell the child the unobserved result in advance.
Have the pupil predict what will differ, observe clarity and flow, record the comparison and explain whether the hypothesis was supported. If still stuck, model only the first inspection step, then return the next choice to the pupil. A lack of visible improvement is still data, not proof that the pupil is incapable. Clarity can indicate fewer suspended particles but cannot establish microbiological or chemical safety.''',
'Even clear water from this filter is not guaranteed safe to drink; no one should taste it.','Clear water from this filter is guaranteed safe to drink.', ['C1','C5'],
'''The student should drink the filtered water to test whether it is safe. Clear appearance proves that all contaminants were removed. Do not ask for a prediction or comparison.''')
